The detection boundary follows a power law in observation length:
\begin{equation}
\alpha_c(T) = 0.193 + 75.68 \cdot T^{-2.41}.
\label{eq:alpha-c}
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Detection phase boundary]\label{prop:phase-boundary}
Under a sub-linear signal model $S(\alpha) = c \cdot \max(0,\, \alpha - \alpha_\infty)^\beta$ with $\beta < 1$, the detection boundary satisfies
\begin{equation}
\alpha_c(T) = \alpha_\infty + A \cdot T^{-1/(2\beta)},
\label{eq:alpha-c-theory}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha_\infty$ is the detection floor, $A = (z^* \sigma / c)^{1/\beta}$, and $z^* = \Phi^{-1}(\pi^*)$ \textup{(proof in Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_c_proof})}.
\end{proposition}

Fitting to Eq.~\ref{eq:alpha-c} yields $\alpha_\infty{=}0.193$, $\beta{=}0.208$, and $\gamma_{\mathrm{theory}} = 1/(2\beta) = 2.408$, recovering the empirical exponent $\gamma{=}2.41$ ($\Delta\gamma{=}0.002$, $R^2{=}1.000$ on 7 points; linear $\beta{=}1$ rejected, $R^2{=}0.711$).
The floor $\alpha_\infty$ means contamination below ${\sim}19\%$ produces no causal signal regardless of $T$; at $T{=}5$, the theory predicts $\alpha_c \geq 1.0$ (no contamination level is detectable), a falsifiable prediction under current experimental verification.
The steepness $k{=}100$ yields a step-like transition; at $T{=}10$, $\alpha_c = 0.487$ (\S\ref{sec:regime}), at $T{=}20$, $\alpha_c = 0.248$ (\S\ref{sec:t20}).
Signal $S(\alpha)$ rises monotonically, but resonance $R(\alpha)$ drops at $\alpha{\approx}0.60$ when canary and downstream responses compress into a single generation.
This yields four regimes: \emph{blind} ($S$ below floor), \emph{onset} ($S$ crosses threshold), \emph{synchronous dip} ($R \to 0$), and \emph{detection} ($S$ dominates).

